WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.824
Raj: The peak load baseline shows sixty-eight percent CPU. Transaction latency remains within target.

2
00:00:07.824 --> 00:00:13.920
Lukas: Which percentile and which request class? An average could hide busy periods.

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.976
Anika: Transaction p ninety-five is one-forty milliseconds. Its target is two hundred milliseconds.

4
00:00:20.976 --> 00:00:28.104
Priya: Just to confirm, reports measured separately, right? They aren't meeting that same target.

5
00:00:28.104 --> 00:00:35.928
Hannah: Correct, reports reach nearly two seconds. Their scans coincide with disk queues.

6
00:00:35.928 --> 00:00:42.528
Raj: We can't rule that out as contention. Maybe a larger CPU won't help enough.

7
00:00:42.528 --> 00:00:48.648
Lukas: I'll keep an eye on I/O ceilings. Instance bandwidth and storage limits differ.

8
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:55.656
Anika: The growth forecast increases both workloads. But it doesn't prove CPU saturation yet.

9
00:00:55.656 --> 00:01:03.600
Hannah: Our working hypothesis is avoidable scan pressure. Let's inspect the query before sizing.

10
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.200
Priya: Okay, keep that distinction in the notes. We need capacity and efficiency together.

